# Intro
One 29.7 Msun star in a uniform, periodic box of cold (50 K), zero-metallicity
molecular gas, with the ISRF transport on (`ISRF_propagation: 1`) and the H2 line
absorption of the transport on (`ISRF_H2_line_absorption: 1`). The star's
Lyman-Werner (LW) light is absorbed by the H2 lines along its path. The check compares,
for every gas particle, the fraction of its own LW energy that the lines let through
(`LWOwnDissociatingSpecificEnergies` over `LWOwnSpecificEnergies`) with the shielding
function at the column `N_H2 = n_H2 r`,
where `n_H2` is the particle's own H2 density and `r` its distance to the star. The
shielding function is the Wolcott-Green and Haiman (2019) fit that geajay uses.

The run is cooling with geajay only: with `ISRF_H2_line_absorption: 1` SWIFT tells the
library not to shield the transported LW rate a second time, so no cooling parameter is
needed for it. Official Grackle refuses `ISRF_H2_line_absorption: 1`.

## What the run holds fixed
The check reads the end of a short run in which H2 and the temperature stay frozen. The
parameters therefore switch off photoionization, stellar winds and supernovae (they would
change the H2 fraction and the temperature near the star), and set the light speed to
`unlimited`. The light crosses one hop per step, so it needs several steps to reach the whole
box. The steps are short (`dt_max` 1.6e-12 in internal units) so that the H2 stays frozen while the
light arrives; the run stops after 8 steps and the check reads the last snapshot. The check refuses a run in which any of these is not set, or in which the H2
density of a domain particle leaves the range of the reference snapshot (see Checking) or the
temperature of a thin one (column below 1e17 cm^-2) changes by more than 5 per cent. The check also refuses a snapshot with a coordinate within 1e-6 L of a box face, and `makeIC.py`
refuses to write one. Only particles at `r < L/2 - 2h` are
read, so that the nearest periodic image of the star is the right one.

# Configure
```
./configure --with-chemistry=GEAR_10 --with-feedback=GEAR --with-cooling=geajay_3 --with-stars=GEAR --with-sink=GEAR --with-star-formation=GEAR --with-tracers=GEAR --with-kernel=wendland-C2 --with-geajay=path/to/geajay --with-chealpix=path/to/chealpix
make -j
```
The geajay build must provide the option that leaves the transported LW rate out of its H2
shielding; without it SWIFT stops at start-up. The packet
transport needs a radiation table whose `Data/Radiation` group carries
`MeanPhotonEnergyPE` and `Integrated_MeanPhotonEnergyPE`; see the README of
`ISRFH2Photodissociation` for how to obtain it.

# Run
```
./run.sh                      # main leg, then the check
leg=switch_off ./run.sh       # line absorption off: the check must stop on the missing line fields
leg=stale_key ./run.sh        # an old GeajayCooling:h2_transported_lw_rate_shielding on the command line has no effect
ranks=2 run_name=two_ranks ./run.sh
leg=restart ./run.sh          # light speed 0.99 c (params_restart.yml), stops after step 17
```
`level`, `gas_density`, `gas_mass`, `h2_ratio` (initial `nH2/nH`) and `n_threads`
override the defaults. The default `h2_ratio` of 3e-4 gives columns from 1e17 to
about 2e18 cm^-2 over the box.

# Checking
`checkH2Attenuation.py` reads `used_parameters.yml`, `fixture.json` (written by
`makeIC.py`), the first and last snapshot, the SWIFT log (`--startup-log output.log`) and
`timesteps.txt` (`--timesteps`).
SWIFT does not write the LW shielding option to `used_parameters.yml`; the check reads it
from the start-up line `h2_transported_lw_rate_shielding = 1` that SWIFT prints, and calls
the run void if the line is absent or reads 0. `run.sh` passes the log.

The exit class of the check is 0 for a pass, 1 for a failed
bar, 2 for a missing file, field or key, 3 for a non-finite value and 4 for a void run (a
premise or a positive control is not met). A void run never passes. `parity` alone also
returns 5 for a pass without a counters file (see below).

`python3 checkH2Attenuation.py starlit --first snap/snapshot_0001.hdf5 --last snap/snapshot_0009.hdf5 --startup-log output.log --timesteps timesteps.txt`

The reference is `snapshot_0001`, the first snapshot written after the first step and
before any probe is lit; `snapshot_0000` holds the initial species fractions, which the
first step changes in every particle.

- Bar: `|ln(ratio / f)|` at most the larger of `|ln f(n_max r) / f(n r)|` and
  `|ln f(n_min r) / f(n r)|`, with `n_min` and `n_max` the extreme H2 densities of the
  domain particles at the reference and the last snapshot: the carried column is a sum of
  the mean `n_H2` of the two ends of each hop times the path of the hop, with paths that
  add up to `r`. Dissociation only lowers H2, so the
  two ends bound every state between them. The run is void if the `n_H2` of a domain
  particle at the last snapshot lies outside the reference range by more than 1e-3. Taking
  the range over both snapshots makes the bar wider than the range of the last snapshot
  alone (about 0.2 per cent in the median on the default run); the check prints the
  widening.
- Kernel mean. The cooling reads the kernel mean of the own fields, not the own ratio.
  The snapshot field `LWLineTransmissions` is the mean of the neighbours' own ratios
  weighted by `m_j W(r_ij / h_i) E_j`, with `E_j` the own LW energy of neighbour `j` and
  the self term included. Neighbours nearer the star are brighter and more transparent,
  so the mean exceeds `f` at the particle by a few per cent. That is a property of the
  averaging, so the check compares the field with the kernel mean of the prediction, not
  with `f`. All tiers take `E_j` and the own dissociating energy from the snapshot, so
  `E_j` itself is not tested here.
  - Tier 1, identity: `|ln(T / T_offline)|` at most `(n_ngb + 2) 2^-24` plus the
    kernel-edge term (see tier 3) for every particle
    (`n_ngb` the neighbours in its kernel), with `T_offline` the weighted mean above
    computed from the own fields of the snapshot (dissociating mean clamped to the LW
    mean). The float LW sum of the code flushes a product `m_j W E_j` below the smallest
    normal float to zero, depending on the order of the sum; the dissociating sum, in
    double, does not. So the offline `T` is a window: its lower end keeps every product,
    its upper end drops the products below the smallest normal float, and the bar is
    measured from the window. The check prints how many particles can lose more than the
    float term to this. A particle whose products are all below the smallest normal float
    must hold no LW and no dissociating energy. A particle with no lit neighbour must hold
    no LW energy. This tier catches a missing self term, a kernel in `h_j` in place of
    `h_i`, and any change of `T` of 0.5 per cent or more. The weight sum of the mean (its
    normalisation) cancels in `T`: tier 3 tests it.
  - Tier 2, physics: `|ln(T / K)|` at most the largest own bar over the lit
    neighbours plus the float term of tier 1, with `K` the same weighted mean of
    `f(n_H2 r)` of each neighbour. `T / K` is a convex combination of the ratios
    `t_j / f_j`, so the bound holds for a correct mean. This tier catches the prediction
    or the own ratio used in place of the mean. A change of 0.5 per cent and the `h_j`
    kernel are below its resolution: tier 1 catches them.
    Tier 2 skips the particles that can lose more than the float term to the flush.
  - Tier 3, means: `E_mean` (`LWSpecificEnergies`) and `D_mean`
    (`LWDissociatingSpecificEnergies`), which the cooling reads, against the offline
    `sum w E / sum w` and `min(sum w D, sum w E) / sum w`, with the self term, so the weight
    sum is tested. The bar is the float sums, `(2 n_ngb + 4) 2^-24` for `E_mean` and that
    plus `(n_ngb + 3) 2^-24` for `D_mean`, plus the kernel-edge term
    `sum_j |b_j - a_j| rel_j`. Here `b_j` is the share of neighbour `j` in the numerator,
    `a_j` its share of the weight sum and `rel_j` the relative float error of its weight:
    the Horner rounding of the kernel polynomial, the rounding of `t = u - origin` and the
    rounding of `u = r / h`: the force loops cast `x - loc` to float for each particle, with
    `loc` the corner of the cell, so the position error scales with the cell width `C`, not
    with `r`. Per component of `dx` it is at most `3 C 2^-24` (a pair of adjacent cells),
    `3 sqrt(3) C 2^-24` for the vector, and the rest of the arithmetic adds 6 times
    `2^-24` in `u`. The width `C` is the top-level cell size of the snapshot
    (`Cells/Meta-data/size`), an upper bound for every cell of the tree. Near the edge of the support `W` falls
    as `(gamma - u)^4`, so `rel_j` is large there and one lit neighbour can set the mean.
    A change of 0.5 per cent or more of `E_mean` or `D_mean`, of the weight sum, of the
    weights or of the fields, fails wherever the bar is below that; the check prints how
    many particles have such a bar, and the run is void if there are none. A neighbour that
    the code counts or drops within a few 2^-24 of the support edge has a weight below
    1e-26 of `W(0)` and is not accounted for. The LW and dissociating sums are held to the
    same flush window as `T`.
  - Premises, read from the run: the snapshot kernel must be `Wendland C2`, the kernel whose
    polynomial the check holds. The step that ends at the snapshot must have updated every
    gas particle (`--timesteps timesteps.txt`), and the positions must have moved by less
    than one float rounding of `h` in it. Otherwise the run is void.
  - Not covered: the own LW energy `E_j`, and a gather that weights a neighbour by the mass
    of the updated particle in place of its own. All gas particles of this fixture have the
    same mass, so that defect gives the same means.
- The light moves one hop per step, so the lit probes fill a ball. With n the number of
  steps (the ratio of the snapshot times, which must be an integer of at least 2, and which
  counts one hop per step only if all gas sits on one time bin, as in the default run;
  the check does not test it), every
  probe at `r < (n - 1) h_min` must be lit, and no probe may be lit beyond `n gamma h_max`
  (`gamma` the kernel support in units of h). The check fails on a lit count outside these
  bounds and on any unlit probe inside the first radius.
- The count and the minimum of the transmission read the lit probes only; an unlit probe
  carries no transmission. The number of lit particles with a transmission below 0.99 must
  be above 0 and must lie between the numbers of lit particles beyond `r_0.99 + l_hop` and
  beyond `r_0.99 - l_hop`, with `l_hop` the smoothing length of the probe. A counters file from an
  instrumented build (`--counters`, key `mean_hop_path_cm`, which the code does not write)
  replaces it by the mean path of the booked shares.
- The carried column `LWLineH2ColumnDensities` of every lit probe must lie between
  `n_min r (1 - e)` and `n_max r (1 + e)`, with `n_min` and `n_max` as in the first bar
  and `e` the float rounding term of the transmission bar. The band is about 2 per cent
  wide on the default run, so it catches a column that is off by a few per cent.
- The check prints how many particles can detect a doubled H2 column. If that is 0, the
  run is void.

Two runs are compared particle by particle with `parity`, on `LWSpecificEnergies`,
`LWDissociatingSpecificEnergies` and `LWLineH2ColumnDensities` (not only their ratio):
```
python3 checkH2Attenuation.py parity --mode restart --a ref/snap/snapshot_0032.hdf5 --b resumed/snap/snapshot_0032.hdf5 --stop ref/snap/snapshot_0017.hdf5
python3 checkH2Attenuation.py parity --mode ranks --a one/snap/snapshot_0009.hdf5 --b two/snap/snapshot_0009.hdf5 --previous one/snap/snapshot_0008.hdf5 --first one/snap/snapshot_0001.hdf5 --params one/used_parameters.yml
```
- `restart`: every `LW` and `Packet` field must be equal bit for bit (same binary, same
  rank count, one thread, so that the run repeats exactly). Other fields are printed only.
  Some particle dark at `--stop` must be lit at the compared snapshot, else the run is void.
- `ranks`: the float sums run in another order. A particle that held light at
  `--previous` must agree within `n (k 2^-24 + p delta_h)` of its own value, with `n` the
  number of steps (the ratio of the times of `--first` and `--a`), `k` the largest number
  of neighbours in a kernel, `p` the cone power plus 4 and `delta_h` the largest relative
  gap of the smoothing lengths. A particle first lit after `--previous` sits at the light
  front, where its light came through weights near the kernel edge; it is held to the same
  bar times the largest value in its kernel.
- The code does not count the booked shares of other ranks, the probes on another rank or
  the slots that wait at a restart. Without a counters file the check therefore prints
  `REDUCED` and exits with class 5 when the comparison passes: the values agree, but the
  run is not shown to have exercised the transport between ranks or the restore of the
  held light. A counters file (`--counters`, JSON) from an instrumented build restores the
  full check: it must give `probes_on_other_rank` and `foreign_bookings` for `ranks`,
  `held_lw_slots_at_restart` and `refits_after_restart` for `restart`, each above 0, and
  may give `steps` in place of `--first`.

`testCheckH2Attenuation.py WORK_DIR` runs the check on synthetic snapshots, with a planted
error in each case, and prints the exit class of each.
